<p class="lead">
  Teachers handle an enormous volume of small document tasks — combining worksheets into a packet, fixing a sideways scan, shrinking a file to fit a school email system's attachment limit — often on documents that include student names, grades, or other information that shouldn't casually pass through a random third-party server.
</p>

<h2>Building worksheets and packets</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/tools/merge-pdf/">Merge PDF</a> — combine individual worksheet pages, a syllabus, and a reading packet into one document to share or print.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/reorder-pdf/">Reorder PDF Pages</a> — fix the order of a packet assembled from multiple source files.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/add-page-numbers-pdf/">Add Page Numbers to PDF</a> — number a multi-page packet so students can reference "page 4" without confusion.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/delete-pages-pdf/">Remove Pages from PDF</a> — trim a textbook chapter or answer key down to just the pages actually being assigned.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/watermark-pdf/">Watermark PDF</a> — mark an answer key as "TEACHER COPY — DO NOT DISTRIBUTE" before it's stored alongside student-facing materials.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Handling scanned assignments</h2>
<p>
  Scanned student work is one of the most common sources of everyday PDF annoyances — sideways pages, huge file sizes from a phone camera scan, pages in the wrong order after a stack got shuffled.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/tools/rotate-pdf/">Rotate PDF</a> — fix pages scanned sideways or upside down.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/compress-pdf/">Compress PDF</a> — shrink a large scanned assignment before uploading it to a learning management system with a file size limit.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/crop-pdf/">Crop PDF</a> — remove excess margins from a phone-scanned page.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/pdf-to-jpg/">PDF to JPG</a> — pull a single page out of a scanned submission to paste into feedback or a slide.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Forms and permission slips</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/tools/fill-pdf-form/">Fill PDF Form</a> — complete a field trip permission slip or standard district form digitally instead of printing it.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/sign-pdf/">Sign PDF</a> — sign routine administrative paperwork without printing, signing, and re-scanning.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/flatten-pdf/">Flatten PDF</a> — lock a filled-out form's values before submitting it, so it can't be silently altered afterward.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Presentation and handout materials</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/tools/convert-image/">Convert Image</a> — get an image into the right format for a slide deck or a printed handout.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/jpg-to-pdf/">JPG to PDF</a> — combine several photographed pages of handwritten notes or a whiteboard into a single shareable PDF.</li>
  <li><a href="/tools/qr-code-generator/">QR Code Generator</a> — create a QR code linking to a class resource, printed directly onto a handout.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why this matters specifically for classroom documents</h2>
<p>
  Student work and administrative paperwork often includes names, grades, and sometimes more sensitive information (accommodations, disciplinary notes, guardian contact details) covered under FERPA in the US and similar student-privacy regulations elsewhere. Uploading a graded assignment or an IEP-related document to a free online PDF converter — even briefly — means that information passes through infrastructure outside the school's control, with no data processing agreement in place. Running the same operations in the browser instead means nothing about a student ever leaves the device doing the processing.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: verifying it yourself</h2>
<p>
  Open your browser's DevTools (F12) → Network tab, run any keptlocal tool, and confirm no request uploads the file. It's a two-minute check worth doing before making a tool part of a regular workflow that touches student documents.
</p>

